PROJECT FILE SPEC (.tufty)
A tufty project stores the state of certain game variables such that the user 
can exit the game, reopen it, reload the project file, and resume working 
with it as though no interruption had occurred.

Central to understanding the file spec is the distinction the game makes between a
"bitmap" and a "meta_bitmap".

A bitmap is a struct defined as follows:
struct bitmap
{
    // This is the entire bitmap file, including the header
    buffer Buffer;

    // Where the pixels actually live. Just a pointer into
    //      the buffer Buffer above where the pixels start.
    u8 *Pixels;
    int Height, Width, BytesPerPixel, Pitch;
};

The first field of a bitmap, "buffer", is defined as follows:
struct buffer
{
    mem_idx Size;
    u8 *Data;
};

And in a bitmap's "buffer", "Data" points to the first byte of the bitmap
in game memory. When we load a bitmap, we parse the file and extract important
information we need to draw it: Height, Width, BytesPerPixel, and Pitch,
and store them in the struct. Similarly, we also store a pointer
directly to where the pixels live in the bitmap file.

A bitmap struct always lives in a struct called a "meta_bitmap":
struct meta_bitmap
{
    char Filepath[STRING_LEN];
    b32 ReadyToReload;
    u64 LastUpdateTime;
    bitmap Bitmap;
};
In addition to the bitmap itself, we store information used while the
game is running that allows us to reload a bitmap if the user edits it
while the game is running, and add or remove a bitmap from the game
if the user removes it from the game's data directory while
the game is running.

Currently, the variables we need to store upon save be divided into 
two groups: player variables and tile variables.

For player variables, we need to save:
- For each of the four directions the player can face, the number of bitmaps that 
    were loaded for that facing direction
- The filepath for every player facing bitmap that was in memory
- The value of the DrawThis variable for each of the four facing directions. 
PLAYER VARIABLE DEFINITIONS:
"Player facing bitmap": Since the player can face in four directions, we 
have a set of "player facing bitmaps" for each of those directions. 
e.g., the user may load multiple bitmaps for each facing direction and 
switch between them while the game is running.

"DrawThis": And since each facing direction can have multiple bitmaps from
which the user may choose, "DrawThis" is simply an integer representing 
the index for the currently selected bitmap for that facing direction.

For tile variables, we save:
- The number of rows that were in the tile map
- The number of columns that were in the tile map
- The number of tile types that were loaded
- The filepath for each tile type that was loaded
- An array of tile values whose size is equal to the number of rows multiplied by the number of columns. Each
        entry in this array is an integer that indexes into the array of tile values. For each tile,
        the renderer reads this value to determine which tile to display on the screen.
TILE VARIABLE DEFINITIONS:
"Tile type": e.g., a grass tile, a water tile, a right-angled path tile, a straight path tile, etc. Each of
these tile types has its own filepath. Therefore, this file contains an array of N zero-terminated strings; the Nth string
contains the filepath for the Nth tile type. Note that while the the entries in the tile type filepath array are zero-indexed,
when we load them into the game, the 0th filepath in the array must be loaded into the TileTypes array at index 1, because
in the game, a TileValue of 0 indicates an empty tile. 

"Tile values": Following the array of strings containing the tile type filepaths is an array of M s32 values, where
M = NumRows * NumCols = the number of tiles in the tile map represented by this file. Basically, the game has a notion of a "tile"; 
we want to iterate over every tile in the map and read a value and know what picture to draw for it. Each s32 is a value in the range of
0 to (number of tile types). In the game, 0 corresponds to an empty tile; it means we draw nothing for that tile
and the player just sees whatever flat color is the background. A tile value of 1 corresponds to the first tile type, represented by the first
filepath string encountered in the tile type filepath array (noting that this array is actually zero-indexed as described above...
man this shit is hard to define). A value of 2 corresponds to the second filepath in the tile type filepath array, etc.

IMPORTANT! For player bitmaps and tile bitmaps, the zero index in arrays in which they are stored
must always be empty. This is so, e.g., we can store a value of 0 for a tile in the game and
have that mean that the tile is empty.
